  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of tournaments that the golfer wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=10%=0.1$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\u223cB(20,0.1).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.1)^{x}(1-0.1)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the golfer wins at least $2$ tournaments. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+\u2026+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.1)^{0}(0.9)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.9)^{20}+20(0.1)(0.9)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.391\\,747 \\\\  & =0.608\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
